tools.description:
Search the curated Lexware Office API catalog by running a JavaScript async arrow function.

Use this before execute to discover endpoints, request shapes, response notes, workflows, and domain-specific caveats.

Available globals:

declare const spec: LexwareApiCatalog;
// Ranked catalog lookup: the compact first step instead of returning whole spec sections.
declare function find(query: string, options?: { limit?: number /* default 8, max 25 */; kinds?: Array<'operation' | 'workflow' | 'semantics'> }): Array<{
  kind: 'operation' | 'workflow' | 'semantics';
  id: string; // operationId, workflow key, or spec.info key
  method?: string; path?: string; // operations only
  summary: string;
  score: number;
  hint: string; // where to read the full entry, e.g. "spec.workflows.fileHandling"
}>;
// Compact view of one operation: summary, description, parameters, requestBody (without shape), responses, notes, docsUrl,
// and fullEntry: where to read the rest. Concrete paths resolve to their template: describe('GET', '/v1/vouchers/<uuid>').
declare function describe(method: string, path: string): object;

Start with find('…'): it ranks operations, workflows and spec.info semantics by your words (English or German, e.g. 'upload beleg', 'umsatz') and returns compact matches with a hint where to read more. Then read only the entries you need with describe(method, path) or the hint path. Return only what you need: avoid returning whole sections such as spec.workflows, spec.paths entries with all their tags, or several spec.info semantics at once — large results are truncated.

Useful starting points:
- spec.info.domainIndex: compact map from business domains to endpoint lists
- spec.info.account: the connected organization — { label?, organizationId, companyName, taxType, smallBusiness, distanceSalesPrinciple?, businessFeatures } or { label?, error } — check it to confirm which organization you are connected to; no need to call /v1/profile
- spec.info.writesEnabled: whether this server currently allows POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE — check before planning writes
- spec.info.writePolicy: { enabled, allow } — allow is 'all' or the only permitted writes, e.g. ['POST /v1/files', 'PUT /v1/vouchers/{id}']
- spec.paths: path -> method -> operation catalog with params, requestBody, responses, examples, capabilities, docsUrl
- spec.workflows: curated recipes for reporting, sales documents, files/uploads, webhooks, and API quirks
- spec.info.voucherStatusSemantics and financeReportingSemantics: finance/status guidance for revenue/Umsatz/profit questions

Sandbox: no network, filesystem, process, fetch, imports, or API key. Return JSON-serializable data; console logs are captured.

Examples:

async () => find('upload beleg')

async () => describe('GET', '/v1/voucherlist')

async () => spec.workflows.fileHandling.steps

async () => Object.entries(spec.info.domainIndex)
  .filter(([name, domain]) => [name, ...domain.tags].some(value => value.toLowerCase().includes('contact')))
  .map(([name, domain]) => ({ name, ...domain }))

Execute a constrained Lexware Office API workflow by running a JavaScript async arrow function.

Use search first when you need endpoint/domain guidance; do not guess Lexware paths from memory. The sandbox exposes no API key, filesystem, process, imports, fetch, or arbitrary network access.

Available globals:

declare const spec: LexwareApiCatalog;
// Ranked catalog lookup: the compact first step instead of returning whole spec sections.
declare function find(query: string, options?: { limit?: number /* default 8, max 25 */; kinds?: Array<'operation' | 'workflow' | 'semantics'> }): Array<{
  kind: 'operation' | 'workflow' | 'semantics';
  id: string; // operationId, workflow key, or spec.info key
  method?: string; path?: string; // operations only
  summary: string;
  score: number;
  hint: string; // where to read the full entry, e.g. "spec.workflows.fileHandling"
}>;
// Compact view of one operation: summary, description, parameters, requestBody (without shape), responses, notes, docsUrl,
// and fullEntry: where to read the rest. Concrete paths resolve to their template: describe('GET', '/v1/vouchers/<uuid>').
declare function describe(method: string, path: string): object;
declare const lexware: {
  request<T = unknown>(input: LexwareRequest): Promise<LexwareResponse<T>>;
  json(input: LexwareRequest): Promise<unknown>;
  paginate<T = unknown>(input: LexwareRequest, options?: { maxPages?: number }): Promise<T[]>;
  requireNumber(row: unknown, fieldPath: string): number;
  requireMoney(row: unknown, fieldPath: string): number;
  sumMoney(rows: unknown[], fieldPath: string): number;
  formatMoney(cents: number, currency?: string): string;
  // GET, shallow-merge patch (read-only fields dropped, files and version kept), PUT, GET to verify: 3 requests. Throws
  // without writing on an expectedVersion mismatch or if files would lose an id (unless allowFileRemoval). Details: spec PUT /v1/vouchers/{id} notes.
  // Dry run: 1 request (the GET); returns the planned PUT and the fields it would change instead of after.
  updateVoucher(id: string, patch: Record<string, unknown>, options?: { expectedVersion?: number; allowFileRemoval?: boolean }): Promise<{ before: unknown; after: unknown; changed: string[] } | { before: unknown; dryRun: true; planned: PlannedWrite; changed: string[] }>;
  // POST /v1/files (type=voucher), then polls the voucher after 2, 4, 8 s until it is no longer blank (timeoutMs default and max 15000):
  // up to 5 requests. One waiting upload per execute call (30 s timeout); for batches pass waitForExtraction: false. Never re-uploads;
  // extraction 'pending' on timeout means GET /v1/vouchers/{voucherId} later. Dry run: no upload, no polling; returns the plan.
  uploadVoucherFile(input: FileInput & { waitForExtraction?: boolean | { timeoutMs?: number } }): Promise<{ fileId: string; voucherId: string; sent: UploadSent; voucher?: unknown; extraction: 'done' | 'pending' | 'skipped' } | { dryRun: true; planned: PlannedWrite; extraction: 'skipped' }>;
  attachFileToVoucher(voucherId: string, input: FileInput): Promise<{ fileId: string; sent: UploadSent } | { dryRun: true; planned: PlannedWrite }>; // POST /v1/vouchers/{id}/files, 1 request
  sleep(ms: number): Promise<void>; // 0-10000 ms per call; the 30 s execute timeout bounds the total
  // Duplicate check before uploads. Exact voucherNumber query (1 request); if no candidate, pages /v1/voucherlist in dateFrom..dateTo (required,
  // else throws): 1 + pages requests; throws if the window has more than maxPages (default 4) pages. Ranks rows by voucherNumber ignoring
  // case/spaces/dashes/dots/leading zeros (8), totalAmount ±0.01 (4), contactName (2), date (1). Never decides; check the candidates.
  findVouchers(criteria: { voucherNumber?: string; contactName?: string | RegExp; amount?: number; currency?: string; dateFrom?: string; dateTo?: string; voucherType?: string; voucherStatus?: string }, options?: { maxPages?: number }): Promise<Array<{ row: unknown; score: number; matched: string[] }>>;
